WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.450
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.910
This episode is about simulating a beautiful
phenomenon in nature, the buckling and coiling

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.070
effect of honey.

00:00:12.070 --> 00:00:13.070
Mmm!

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:17.410
This effect is due to the high viscosity of
materials like honey, which means that they

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.590
are highly resistant against deformation.

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:25.180
Water, however, is much less viscous as it
is held together by weaker intermolecular

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:31.510
forces, therefore it is easier to deform,
making it so easy to pour it into a glass.

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:36.230
We had an earlier episode on honey buckling,
and as every seasoned Fellow Scholar already

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.740
knows, the link is available in the video
description.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:45.010
One key difference of this work is that the
older solution was built upon a Lagrangian

00:00:45.010 --> 00:00:50.120
approach, which means that the simulation
consists of computing the velocities and the

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.760
pressure that acts on these particles.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:54.890
It is a particle-based simulation.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:01:00.370
Here, a solution is proposed for the Eulerian
approach, which means that we do not compute

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:06.170
these quantities everywhere in the continuum
of space, but we use a fine 3D grid, and we

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.390
compute these quantities only in these gridpoints.

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:11.810
No particles to be seen anywhere.

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:16.359
There are mathematical techniques to try to
guess what happens between these individual

00:01:16.359 --> 00:01:20.609
gridpoints, and this process is referred to
as interpolation.

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:25.290
So normally, in this grid-based approach,
if we wish to simulate such a buckling effect,

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:30.490
we'll be sorely disappointed because what
we will see is that the surface details rapidly

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.850
disappear due to the inaccuracies in the simulation.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:38.219
The reason for this is that the classical
grid-based simulators utilize a technique

00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:41.069
that mathematicians like to call operator
splitting.

00:01:41.069 --> 00:01:46.671
This means that we solve these fluid equations
by taking care of advection, pressure, and

00:01:46.671 --> 00:01:49.079
viscosity separately.

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.950
Separate quantities, separate solutions.

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:56.840
This is great, because it eases the computational
complexity of the problem, however, we have

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:01.419
to pay a price for it in the form of newly
introduced inaccuracies.

00:02:01.419 --> 00:02:06.579
For instance, some kinetic and shear forces
are significantly dampened, which leads to

00:02:06.579 --> 00:02:10.599
a loss of detail for buckling effects with
traditional techniques.

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:16.420
This paper introduces a new way of efficiently
solving these operators together in a way

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.849
that these coupling effects are retained in
the simulation.

00:02:19.849 --> 00:02:24.650
The final solution not only looks stable,
but is mathematically proven to work well

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:29.819
for a variety of cases, and it also takes
into consideration collisions with other solid

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:31.180
objects correctly.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.819
I absolutely love this, and anyone who is
in the middle of creating a new movie with

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:40.019
some fluid action going on has to be all over
this new technique.

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:42.579
And, the paper is absolutely amazing.

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:49.439
It contains crystal clear writing, many paragraphs
are so tight that I'd find it almost impossible

00:02:49.439 --> 00:02:55.400
to cut even one word from them, yet it is
still digestible and absolutely beautifully

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.400
written.

00:02:56.400 --> 00:03:00.270
Make sure to have a look, as always, the link
is available in the video description.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.629
These amazing papers are stories that need
to be told to everyone.

00:03:04.629 --> 00:03:06.019
Not only to experts.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.060
To everyone.

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.989
And before creating these videos, I always
try my best to be in contact with the authors

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:13.180
of these works.

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.480
And nowadays, many of them are telling me
that they were really surprised by the influx

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.819
of views they got after they were showcased
in the series.

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:26.030
Writing papers that are featured in Two Minute
Papers takes a ridiculous amount of hard work,

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:30.819
and after that, the researchers make them
available for everyone free of charge.

00:03:30.819 --> 00:03:35.989
And now, I am so glad to see them get more
and more recognition for their hard work.

00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:37.069
Absolutely amazing.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:41.400
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

